--TEST--
An array key that arrives as a `mixed` is normalized from its own tag — a `string` as itself, an `int` or a `uint` as its decimal — and every other tag throws where a declared type would have been refused at the site (`rule:types/unions-and-mixed`, `rule:types/arrays`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/mixed

class Key {
    // `rule:types/unions-and-mixed`: `mixed` is the one position nothing is
    // checked at, so a key slot defers to the value's own tag exactly as an
    // operand or a subscript base does. `rule:types/arrays` is what the tag is
    // read against, and the kinds it refuses throw here rather than at the
    // site, because the site could not see them.
    public static function read(array<string> $t, mixed $k): string {
        try {
            return $t[$k];
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }
}

array<string> $t = ["8" => "eight", "k" => "v"];

// The three kinds `rule:types/arrays` accepts: a `string` is the key already,
// and an `int` or a `uint` normalizes to its own decimal — the same key
// `$t[8]` reaches where the subscript's type is visible.
mixed $text = "k";
mixed $whole = 8;
mixed $unsigned = 8 as uint;
echo Key::read($t, $text), "\n";
echo Key::read($t, $whole), "\n";
echo Key::read($t, $unsigned), "\n";
echo $t[8], "\n";

// And the kinds it refuses, which `E0434` names at the site wherever the
// declared type shows them.
mixed $fraction = 1.5;
mixed $flag = true;
mixed $nothing = null;
mixed $table = ["x"];
echo Key::read($t, $fraction), "\n";
echo Key::read($t, $flag), "\n";
echo Key::read($t, $nothing), "\n";
echo Key::read($t, $table), "\n";

// Every key site takes that one normalization: a write, an `unset`, an array
// literal's explicit key, and a subscript whose base is itself a `mixed`.
array<string> $w = ["kept" => "yes"];
$w[$text] = "written";
echo Core\Arr::count($w), "\n";
unset($w[$text]);
echo Core\Arr::count($w), "\n";
array<string> $lit = [$text => "in a literal", $whole => "and a number"];
echo $lit["k"], " / ", $lit["8"], "\n";
mixed $base = $t;
echo $base[$text], " ", $base[$whole], "\n";
--EXPECT--
v
eight
eight
eight
`float` is not an array key — array keys are `int`, `uint`, or `string`
`bool` is not an array key — array keys are `int`, `uint`, or `string`
`null` is not an array key — array keys are `int`, `uint`, or `string`
`array<T>` is not an array key — array keys are `int`, `uint`, or `string`
2
1
in a literal / and a number
v eight
